{
  "operations": [
    {
      "op": "update",
      "collection": "processes",
      "target_id": "PROC_RELAXATION",
      "fields": {
        "scientific_name": "Cessation of ACh-driven signalling, repolarization, SR Ca2+ reuptake and reshielding of actin-binding sites",
        "preconditions": {
          "motor_neuron_stops_ACh_release": true,
          "ATP_available_for_pumps": true
        },
        "effects": {
          "sarcolemma_repolarized": true,
          "ca_returned_to_SR": true,
          "actin_binding_sites_reshielded": true,
          "muscle_fibre_relaxed": true
        },
        "temporal_scope": {
          "start_condition": "Motor neuron stops releasing ACh (normal pathway); alternatively ATP depletion/fatigue can terminate contraction without normal relaxation",
          "end_condition": "Actin-binding sites reshielded and fibre loses tension (normal pathway)",
          "after": ["PROC_CONTRACTION_CROSSBRIDGE_CYCLE"]
        },
        "exceptions": [
          {
            "description": "A muscle also can stop contracting when it runs out of ATP and becomes fatigued; this ATP-depletion route is not modelled here as normal relaxation and does not produce the ST_FIBRE_RELAXED effect.",
            "evidence_quote": "A muscle also can stop contracting when it runs out of ATP and becomes fatigued ( Figure 10.9 ).",
            "location": "paragraph_3"
          }
        ],
        "support": [
          {
            "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
            "quote": "Muscle contraction usually stops when signaling from the motor neuron ends, which repolarizes the sarcolemma and T-tubules, and closes the voltage-gated calcium channels in the SR.",
            "location": "paragraph_3"
          },
          {
            "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
            "quote": "ATP-driven pumps will move Ca ++ out of the sarcoplasm back into the SR. This results in the “reshielding” of the actin-binding sites on the thin filaments. Without the ability to form cross-bridges between the thin and thick filaments, the muscle fiber loses its tension and relaxes.",
            "location": "paragraph_26"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "op": "update",
      "collection": "relations",
      "target_id": "REL_CA_TO_TROPONIN_TROPOMYOSIN",
      "fields": {
        "definition": "Ca2+ released into the sarcoplasm binds troponin, causing tropomyosin to slide away and expose myosin-binding sites on actin",
        "source_entity": "ENT_TROPONIN_TROPOMYOSIN",
        "target_entity": "ENT_ACTIN_MYOSIN_SARCOMERE",
        "support": [
          {
            "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
            "quote": "The first step in the process of contraction is for Ca ++ to bind to troponin so that tropomyosin can slide away from the binding sites on the actin strands.",
            "location": "paragraph_11"
          }
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "op": "update",
      "collection": "processes",
      "target_id": "PROC_CONTRACTION_CROSSBRIDGE_CYCLE",
      "fields": {
        "observables": [
          {
            "constraint_id": "OBS_CONTRACTION_REQUIRES_CA_AND_ATP",
            "predicate": "relation_holds_during",
            "relation_ref": "REL_CROSSBRIDGE_ACTIN_MYOSIN",
            "source_role": "contractile_filaments",
            "target_role": "contractile_filaments",
            "capability": "PARTIAL",
            "evidence_ids": ["E_d6fd3723dbb7"],
            "support": [
              {
                "evidence_id": "E_d6fd3723dbb7",
                "quote": "As long as Ca ++ ions remain in the sarcoplasm to bind to troponin, which keeps the actin-binding sites “unshielded,” and as long as ATP is available to drive the cross-bridge cycling and the pulling of actin strands by myosin, the muscle fiber will continue to shorten to an anatomical limit.",
                "location": "paragraph_1"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ],
